<!--
  consultation-block.html.template — the consultation component set for a route B
  report that has to be ANSWERED rather than read.

  See references/02-local-first-artifacts.md § 8. Copy the blocks you need into
  the page body. There is no <style> and no <script> here any more: wrap-report.sh
  injects artifact-kit's tokens.css, components.css and composer.js, so writing a
  page is writing content plus class names. A local <style> is for the one rule
  this page genuinely needs that the kit does not have.

  THE ONE RULE THAT MATTERS: `data-id` is assigned once and never renumbered.
  Inserting a claim appends a new id; it does not shift the existing ones. A
  reply that says "on 3 I disagree" has to still mean the same claim after the
  next regeneration, and renumbering is what silently breaks that.

  Every item needs a title, a reply surface — radio, checkbox, select, short
  text — AND a notes box. The choice surface is your pick; the notes box is not
  optional on any of them. A closed list carries the answer the author
  anticipated and loses the one they did not, so the reader ends up picking the
  nearest wrong option instead of saying what they mean. The contract check
  fails an item without one.

  The page-level general-notes item is the same rule one level up, and it is
  additional: per-item notes do not replace it.

  Every item also gets a per-item "Clear" control, injected by the composer in
  the page's own language. Do not write one into a block: it arrives by wrapping,
  so a page cannot ship without it and an author cannot get it wrong.

  Depth is set by the cost of undoing the decision: a reversible one-minute
  choice gets the question alone; something touching code or a shared contract
  gets a concrete example; something that rewrites what exists gets the example,
  the consequence of each option, and the file it touches.

  THE UNIT IS THE BLOCK, NOT THE ITEM (BL-247). A block is one context with the
  decisions that fall out of it — one decision or several — and it is the ONLY
  place an item may live: `<section class="consult-group" id="G1" data-id="G1"
  data-title="…">` holding the shared evidence (prose, a table, a slice of the
  drawing) above its `consult-item`s. The general-notes item is the one item
  outside any block. Before the first block only the header (title +
  standfirst), a figure section and the ledger may appear; between blocks,
  nothing; reference material (where the figures come from) goes AFTER the
  notes. check-artifact.sh FAILS a page that breaks any of that — the shape is
  a fact of the DOM, unlike item quality, which stays the rule you hold.

  THE BLOCK IS SELF-SUFFICIENT. The test: could the reader answer every decision
  in it if the page began at the block's heading? What the decisions share goes
  in the block's context; what only one needs (its example, its options, its
  recommendation) goes in the item. Reported from use, twice — "me obliga a
  entender arriba y a responder abajo" and, on a page whose items were long and
  whose preamble was 1,553 words, "tengo que seguir viendo arriba".

  The shape each item follows: evidence -> options with hints -> recommendation.
  A block's id (G1, G2…) is as stable as an item's: --prev holds it too.

  AND THE RECOMMENDATION IS VISIBLE. An item with options says which one the
  session backs, through `data-recommended` on that option's input. Never by
  typing "(recommended)" into `data-label`: that attribute is what the composer
  copies, so the marker reaches the pasted reply and never reaches the page.
  check-artifact.sh warns when it finds one there.

  OPTION GROUPS LIVE IN `.opts`, AND ONLY THERE. `class="opts one"` for a radio
  group, `class="opts"` for checkboxes. components.css styles options under no
  other class, so a group in a wrapper of your own invention renders with no
  grid, no hover and its hints inline — and passes the contract, which checks
  ids, notes boxes and buttons. That is a warning now, not a silence.
-->

<!--
  A consultation opens with the DRAWING when the subject has a shape — a flow, a
  layout, two alternatives to compare — and the prose explains it. Inline SVG and
  mermaid both work offline, which the contract requires. Declare which case this
  page is; a page that carries neither a visual nor a reason fails the check,
  because the reader asked for the picture nine times over 90 days and got it
  every time, so the ask kept coming back (USAGE-19).

  Replace `none:` with `svg` / `mermaid` / `img` as soon as the page has one.

  The placeholder below DOES NOT SATISFY the check — that is deliberate. It used
  to: a "reason" that is the instruction to write a reason passed the gate, so
  every page derived from this template shipped with the visual question already
  answered by nobody. The check now rejects it by name, which means a page copied
  from here fails until someone decides. That failure is the template working.
-->
<meta name="consult-visual" content="none: replace this with the reason, or with svg/mermaid/img">

<!-- A BLOCK: the context, then the decisions that fall out of it. Copy the
     whole block; a single decision still gets its own block. -->
<section class="consult-group" id="G1" data-id="G1" data-title="Short name for this context">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Block G1 &middot; what this is about</p>
    <h2>The context, stated as a finding</h2>
  </div>
  <p>The evidence the decisions below share: the numbers, the paths, the slice
     of the visual. Everything the reader needs to answer every item in this
     block without scrolling anywhere else.</p>

<!-- Single choice. `data-label` is what the composed reply says; without it the
     paste carries the input value, which is usually a bare id.

     `data-recommended` on ONE input marks the option this session backs: the kit
     draws a visible pill next to its title AND appends "(recommended)" to the
     copied label, from that one attribute. `data-recommended="no"` marks an
     option explicitly advised against (a muted, dashed pill). Optional is simply
     the absence of the attribute — do not invent a third value. On a checkbox
     group every marked option is a recommendation. -->
<section class="consult-item" data-id="c1" data-title="Short claim title">
  <h3><span class="consult-id">c1</span>The question, asked as a question</h3>
  <p>The finding, and the evidence for it: the number, the path, the slice of the
     visual. Everything the reader needs to answer without scrolling back up.</p>
  <p>What is at stake, and what each answer costs.</p>
  <div class="opts one">
    <label><input type="radio" name="c1" data-label="First option" data-recommended><span>First option <span class="hint">What it buys, and what it costs — and, on this one, why it is the recommendation.</span></span></label>
    <label><input type="radio" name="c1" data-label="Second option"><span>Second option <span class="hint">What it buys, and what it costs.</span></span></label>
    <label><input type="radio" name="c1" data-label="Third option" data-recommended="no"><span>Third option <span class="hint">Why this one is advised against.</span></span></label>
  </div>
  <p class="fieldlabel">Notes on this one</p>
  <textarea placeholder="Anything the options do not cover…"></textarea>
</section>

<!-- Multiple choice. Same markup, `type="checkbox"`; the composer lists every
     checked label. Drop `.one` to let short labels lay out in columns. -->
<section class="consult-item" data-id="c2" data-title="Which of these apply">
  <h3><span class="consult-id">c2</span>Which of these apply?</h3>
  <div class="opts">
    <label><input type="checkbox" name="c2a" data-label="First"><span>First</span></label>
    <label><input type="checkbox" name="c2b" data-label="Second"><span>Second</span></label>
    <label><input type="checkbox" name="c2c" data-label="Third"><span>Third</span></label>
  </div>
  <p class="fieldlabel">Notes on this one</p>
  <textarea placeholder="Anything the options do not cover…"></textarea>
</section>

<!-- Select, for a closed list too long to lay out as options. The composer sends
     the selected option's TEXT, so the first entry must not read as an answer. -->
<section class="consult-item" data-id="c3" data-title="Pick one from the list">
  <h3><span class="consult-id">c3</span>Pick one from the list</h3>
  <p class="fieldlabel">The choice</p>
  <select>
    <option value="">— not answered —</option>
    <option>First</option>
    <option>Second</option>
  </select>
  <p class="fieldlabel">Notes on this one</p>
  <textarea placeholder="Anything the list does not cover…"></textarea>
</section>

<!-- Short text, for a value rather than an opinion: a name, a number, a path. -->
<section class="consult-item" data-id="c4" data-title="A single value">
  <h3><span class="consult-id">c4</span>A single value</h3>
  <p class="fieldlabel">The value</p>
  <input type="text" placeholder="e.g. 15rem">
  <p class="fieldlabel">Notes on this one</p>
  <textarea placeholder="Anything the value alone does not say…"></textarea>
</section>

</section>
<!-- end of block G1 -->

<!-- The page-level general-notes item. Always last, always present: it is where
     the reply that does not fit any question goes, and without it that reply is
     lost or forced into the wrong item. -->
<section class="consult-item consult-notes" data-id="notes" data-title="General notes">
  <h3><span class="consult-id">notes</span>General notes</h3>
  <p class="fieldlabel">Anything that does not fit above</p>
  <textarea placeholder="Whatever it is…"></textarea>
</section>

<!-- The copy button appears TWICE, sharing one status string: once at the end of
     the content, and once in the rail. A reader who finishes answering and finds
     no button where the answers ended has been failed by the page.

     The ids are not interchangeable. The contract greps for `id="consult-copy"`
     and `id="consult-status"` literally; the `-end` pair does not satisfy it. -->
<div class="endbar">
  <button type="button" id="consult-copy-end">Copy my answers</button>
  <span class="consult-status" id="consult-status-end"></span>
</div>

<!-- …and this one lives inside the page's <aside class="rail">, after the
     <nav class="raillist" id="raillist"></nav> the composer fills in. -->
<div class="consult-bar">
  <button type="button" id="consult-copy">Copy my answers</button>
  <span class="consult-status" id="consult-status"></span>
</div>
